  "response": "**The most dangerous thing here is `sendPayout` in `src/legacy/payouts.ts:23-42`, scan finding `crime_00007` (\"Double Jeopardy\").** It retries a money transfer up to five times with nothing that lets the receiver spot a repeat. The empty `catch` beside it (`crime_00011`) makes it worse.\n\n## The evidence\n\nFrom the scan:\n> `retry construct: attempt loop: while (attempt < 5) (line 25)` · `retried mutation — fetch at line 28 (HTTP POST)` · `missing safeguard (primary): no idempotency or deduplication key` · `also absent: error classification, delay or backoff, jitter, timeout or cancellation`\n\nFrom the source, which makes it worse than the scan says:\n\n```ts\nwhile (attempt < 5) {\n  attempt += 1;\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(\"https://payouts.internal/v1/send\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        destination: payout.destination,\n        amountCents: payout.amountCents,\n        at: Date.now(),\n      }),\n    });\n    if (res.ok) return true;\n  } catch {\n    // swallowed: the caller cannot tell a failure from a refusal\n  }\n}\nreturn false;\n```\n\nThree things the scan doesn't spell out:\n1. **`payout.id` is never sent.** The function has a unique ID for the payout and drops it. The request carries only where the money goes and how much.\n2. **`at: Date.now()` changes on every attempt.** Each retry has a different body, so the provider can't even match two identical requests to catch a duplicate.\n3. **Any response that isn't OK triggers another send**, including 4xx errors and responses that mean \"accepted, still processing\". There's no wait between attempts. The file's own TODO says so: `// TODO: this needs an idempotency key before the next audit`.\n\n## What a customer would experience\n\nSay the payout provider commits the transfer, but the response is lost to a timeout or a dropped connection. `fetch` throws, the empty `catch` hides it, and the loop sends the same amount again right away. **One payout can go out as many as five times.** The caller may still get `false`, so the system records \"payout failed\" while the money left several times. The likely next step is a manual re-send, which makes it worse.\n\nYour finance team then has to claw money back from customers or merchants. The customer sees duplicate deposits that later get reversed, and they may already have spent the money. Nothing is logged, so nobody finds out until reconciliation or the customer calls.\n\n## Why it beats the findings at the top of the scan\n\nThe scan ranks this low partly because of recency: it has `recency: 0` and severity 0.54 (\"medium\"). The top entries have `recency: 1` because the team is working on those files now. Judged by harm instead:\n\n| Finding | Why it ranks below the payout retry |\n|---|---|\n| **crime_00001** `Basket` contract drift (high, #1) | This is two type declarations that disagree. It can let a discount or currency field get dropped in a later change, but it's a compile-time mismatch in code that is actively maintained and tested. It doesn't send money anywhere, and a pricing error can be fixed before or after checkout. |\n| **crime_00004** mixed UTC/local dates in `reconcile.ts` (high) | Records near midnight can land on the wrong day in reconciliation. That's a reporting error you can fix and re-run, and no money moves. |\n| **crime_00005** duplicated admin/plan rule (high) | Both copies are identical today: `actor.role === \"admin\" && actor.plan !== \"free\"`. The risk is that they drift apart in the future, but it isn't wrong right now. |\n| **crime_00008** `settleBatch` retry (same type) | This is the closest rival, and still less dangerous. It sends `ledgerId`, which the provider could use to deduplicate. It only retries on 502/503/504 or a thrown error, not on every non-OK response. And it records `settledAt`. The payout path has none of those safeguards. |\n| **crime_00017** swallowed checkout error, the TODOs, the weak tests | A failed session creation shows up straight away as a broken checkout, and nobody loses money. |\n\n**The ranking rule:** this is the only finding where one ordinary network fault, with no code change and no developer mistake, sends real money out more than once with no record of it. The money goes outside the company and can't be reversed by a code fix. The code has no tests (`test_gap: 0.75`), no owner (`// TODO: nobody has owned this file since the migration`), and nobody is looking at it, which is why the recency weighting buries it. It will only come up again when it goes wrong.\n\n**The fix:** send `payout.id` as an `Idempotency-Key` header on every attempt. Drop `at` from the body, or make it the same on every attempt. Retry only on 5xx errors or network errors, with backoff in between. Log the error in the `catch` and re-throw it.",
